.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="S. F. Boys (1950) proposed building molecular wavefunctions from Gaussian functions e^{-\alpha r^2} instead of the physically natural Slater-type e^{-\zeta r}. The payoff is the Gaussian product theorem: the product of two Gaussians on different centres is a single Gaussian on a point between them, so every multi-centre integral reduces to a closed form. This example checks the theorem numerically, checks the closed-form overlap integral (~chemistrykit.quantum.utils.basis_sets.overlap_integral) and the Boys function (~chemistrykit.quantum.utils.basis_sets.boys_f0) against brute-force quadrature, and shows the price paid: one Gaussian has no cusp at the nucleus, while a contraction of three (STO-3G) comes close to the Slater 1s orbital.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_04_boys_gaussian_orbitals_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/plot_04_boys_gaussian_orbitals`

